Author: Danny Milosavljevic <dannym@friendly-machines.com>
Date: 2026-04-25
Subject: Keep scanner ownership on compiled modules and preserve reload locals
License: expat

FPC 1.0.6 uses `tmodule.scanner' as a loose cache/ownership slot across
reset, nested compile, and second-load reload paths. Native FPC mostly got
away with this because the freed scanner memory often remained untouched,
but the tp2cc-translated bootstrap makes two real failures visible:

 * stale freed scanner pointers kept reachable from module records, and
 * the second-load `loadunit' path reading an uninitialized local
   `scanner' after `reset' nilled the module field.

Fix the lifetime rules explicitly:

 * clear `tmodule.scanner' when the scanner lifetime ends,
 * keep scanner ownership on `compiled_module' instead of whichever
   module `current_module' happens to point at during nested loads, and
 * preserve the reload path's old scanner only in the local helper
   variable used by `loadppufile'.

--- a/compiler/files.pas
+++ b/compiler/files.pas
@@ -1213,6 +1213,7 @@
       begin
         if assigned(scanner) then
           pscannerfile(scanner)^.invalid:=true;
+        scanner:=nil;
         if assigned(globalsymtable) then
           begin
             dispose(punitsymtable(globalsymtable),done);
@@ -1394,6 +1395,7 @@
         _exports:=nil;
         if assigned(scanner) then
           pscannerfile(scanner)^.invalid:=true;
+        scanner:=nil;
         if assigned(sourcefiles) then
          dispose(sourcefiles,done);
         sourcefiles:=nil;
@@ -1583,4 +1585,4 @@
   Revision 1.100  1999/08/24 13:14:01  peter
     * MEMDEBUG to see the sizes of asmlist,asmsymbols,symtables
 
-}
\ No newline at end of file
